    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Double-Sided Cards Replace the Birdfeeder</h2>

  <p>
    The cleverest change: each of the 106 cards has a bird on one side and a
    food type on the other. A central market shows four cards bird-side up and
    four food-side up. When you take food, you're literally taking a card that
    could have been someone's bird — and vice versa. The birdfeeder's dice
    randomness is gone, replaced by a shared market of tough choices.
  </p>

  <h2>No Rounds, No Round-End Goals</h2>

  <p>
    Original Wingspan is paced by four rounds with decreasing turns and
    round-end goal scoring. Pocket drops the structure entirely: players simply
    alternate turns, and the game ends when someone plays their sixth bird.
    Nine optional goal cards are included for advanced play, but the default
    game is a straight race to build the best small engine.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Eggs Are Still Here">
    The egg economy survived the shrink: 60 egg tokens (light pink with gold flecks) still fuel plays and scoring — they just live in a much smaller box. If you like your components tidy, the box has no insert at all, which is exactly why we designed one.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Is It a Replacement for Wingspan?</h2>

  <p>
    No — and it isn't trying to be. Pocket is the game you bring on a train,
    to a café, or to the table with someone who'd bounce off the full game's
    setup. The original remains the deeper engine-builder; Pocket is the one
    that's always within reach. Most Wingspan households will end up owning
    both for different moments.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="info" title="Release Info">
    Wingspan Pocket launched July 15, 2026 on the Stonemaier webstore, with retail availability from late August 2026. It's standalone — you don't need original Wingspan to play — and features birds from around the world rather than one region.
  </InfoBox>
</Article>
